**Cause:** The callback you pass to `setInterval` is a standard `function`, which determines its own `this` value based on how it gets invoked — not based on the surrounding class context. Since `setInterval` calls the function as a plain function call, `this` inside that function doesn't refer to the `Timer` instance.

In strict mode (which class bodies automatically use), a plain function call doesn't allow `this` to fall back to the global object — instead, `this` remains `undefined`. When you then try to execute `this.seconds += 1`, you're attempting to access the `.seconds` property on `undefined`, which throws a `TypeError`.

(If this function were defined outside a class or strict-mode context, `this` would default to the global object, so `this.seconds` would evaluate to `undefined`, producing `undefined + 1 = NaN` with each interval tick — this is likely what you've seen elsewhere.)

**Fix:** Replace the regular function with an arrow function. Arrow functions don't create their own `this` binding — instead, they adopt the `this` value from their lexical parent scope (in this case, the `start()` method, where `this` is the `Timer` instance):

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

Alternative solutions include: binding the function explicitly with `.bind(this)`, or saving a reference to `this` with `const self = this;` before defining the callback.

