## Cause

The issue stems from how `this` behaves within a regular function.

When a regular `function () {...}` is passed to `setInterval`, JavaScript executes that function independently, without binding it to any class instance. Because of this, `this` points to the global object rather than your `Timer` instance. Since the global object lacks a `seconds` property, `this.seconds` evaluates to `undefined`. When you add 1 to `undefined`, the result is `NaN`, which explains why `NaN` appears in the console every second.

## Fix

Replace the regular function with an arrow function. Arrow functions don't have their own `this` binding—instead, they inherit `this` from their enclosing scope, which in this situation is your `Timer` instance.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

With this change, `this` correctly references the `Timer` instance, allowing `this.seconds` to increment properly and log the sequence `1`, `2`, `3`, and beyond.

